      "text_raw": "The precise transconductor\nanalyzed before, has two\noutputs, one at the Drain as\nbefore, but also one at the\nSource of the output transis-\ntor. What is the difference?\nThe first difference is that\nthese two outputs have\nopposite polarities. Also, the\nloop gain is the same for\nboth, but not the actual\nclosed-loop voltage gains.\nThe first one A , at the\nv1\nDrain, is larger.\nThe output resistances are\nalso very different. The\nsecond one R , at the Source, is much smaller, because it is only of the order of magnitude\nOUT2\nof 1/g and it involves the loop gain LG. Connection of a capacitive load at the second output\nm\nwould give a pole at high frequencies only.",
      "text": "The precise transconductor analyzed before, has two outputs, one at the Drain as before, but also one at the Source of the output transistor. What is the difference? The first difference is that these two outputs have opposite polarities. Also, the loop gain is the same for both, but not the actual closed-loop voltage gains. The first one A , at the v1 Drain, is larger. The output resistances are also very different. The second one R , at the Source, is much smaller, because it is only of the order of magnitude OUT2 of 1/g and it involves the loop gain LG. Connection of a capacitive load at the second output m would give a pole at high frequencies only."
    }
